# 7. EXAMPLE: PROCUREMENT AUTOMATIC WORKFLOW

The system must implement the following connected workflow:

- Department raises Purchase Requisition.
- PR goes to the configured approval authority.
- Approved PR becomes available for RFQ.
- RFQ is sent to selected suppliers.
- Supplier quotations are entered.
- Comparative Statement automatically compares quotations.
- CS goes through approval.
- Approved CS allows Purchase Order creation.
- PO is generated using approved CS and PR data.
- PO goes through its configured approval levels.
- Approved PO becomes available to Security / Gate Management.
- Material arrives and IGP is generated.
- IGP automatically references the PO.
- Inspection is performed against the IGP and PO.
- Inspection result determines accepted and rejected quantities.
- Approved inspection allows GRN generation.
- GRN automatically updates accepted stock in Inventory.
- GRN creates the relevant Goods Received / Inventory Accounting reference.
- Supplier invoice is matched against PO and GRN.
- Invoice verification checks quantity, rate, tax and amount differences.
- Approved invoice creates Accounts Payable / Purchase Voucher according to accounting configuration.
- Payment is processed through Payment Voucher.
- Payment updates supplier outstanding and cash/bank ledger.
- Complete history remains available from PR to final payment.

### Mandatory controls

- No GRN without a valid IGP and inspection reference, unless an authorized exception workflow is configured.
- No invoice approval beyond configured quantity/rate tolerances without authorized approval.
- No duplicate supplier invoice.
- No payment without an approved payable transaction.
- Rejected or cancelled documents must not update stock or ledgers.
- Partial deliveries and multiple GRNs against one PO must be supported.
- One PO may have multiple deliveries.
- One GRN may contain multiple items.
- Every linked document must be accessible through a document-flow screen.

